{"h":3396,"w":2400,"lines":[{"t":"Test 1","y":0.033430232417226202,"x":0.084362141302483054,"c":1,"h":0.01309509131315878,"w":0.059670778910319008},{"t":"READING","y":0.079335106072915607,"w":0.13405132293701177,"c":1,"h":0.018655369222795981,"x":0.4340031450127238},{"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","x":0.082304522250554582,"h":0.01598837238037687,"y":0.14098837194390823,"w":0.30658437093098961,"c":1},{"y":0.17595442122440164,"c":1,"w":0.8171988932291665,"h":0.023372458063671497,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","x":0.082139704878251701},{"y":0.19611307445406512,"x":0.082304528429576751,"t":"Passage 1 below.","w":0.16872427622477215,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1},{"h":0.026813844067469117,"w":0.53111663818359367,"x":0.2364880009562442,"y":0.23973655097123492,"c":1,"t":"Why we need to protect polar bears"},{"w":0.82939229329427078,"h":0.01969982906561718,"c":1,"x":0.080153428194349771,"y":0.28113324700363274,"t":"Polar bears are being increasingly threatened by the effects of climate change, but their disappearance"},{"w":0.77581644694010421,"y":0.29932392558238463,"c":1,"h":0.018388816689714815,"x":0.080191432613461303,"t":"could have far-reaching consequences. They are uniquely adapted to the extreme conditions of"},{"x":0.080246901832165166,"t":"the Arctic Circle, where temperatures can reach -40°C. One reason for this is that they have up to","w":0.79835393269856769,"h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1,"y":0.31831395334206558},{"y":0.33396945925463406,"c":1,"t":"11 centimetres of fat underneath their skin. Humans with comparative levels of adipose tissue would","w":0.82323633829752596,"h":0.019025038774218461,"x":0.080136257538335282},{"t":"be considered obese and would be likely to suffer from diabetes and heart disease. Yet the polar bear","w":0.81911697387695315,"h":0.019220606036686072,"x":0.080142176131450929,"y":0.35172975924276606,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.078189302281420653,"t":"experiences no such consequences.","y":0.37209302311288095,"w":0.29218106587727866,"h":0.01453830777405285},{"x":0.080143262346165228,"y":0.40377775212379596,"t":"A 2014 study by Shi Ping Liu and colleagues sheds light on this mystery. They compared the genetic","w":0.82529790242513035,"c":1,"h":0.021294460139370086},{"w":0.80673334757486981,"t":"structure of polar bears with that of their closest relatives from a warmer climate, the brown bears.","h":0.019552019656196018,"x":0.078122097208425126,"c":1,"y":0.42270140022558866},{"x":0.080246921939862825,"w":0.77777776082356764,"y":0.44186046495690601,"t":"This allowed them to determine the genes that have allowed polar bears to survive in one of the","h":0.017503492402524912,"c":1},{"y":0.45676265370343316,"t":"toughest environments on Earth. Liu and his colleagues found the polar bears had a gene known as","w":0.81091186523437508,"h":0.021434900757842379,"c":1,"x":0.078081797883793908},{"w":0.78806584676106772,"y":0.47673733830322051,"h":0.017448708362377108,"t":"APoB, which reduces levels of low-density lipoproteins (LDLs) - a form of 'bad' cholesterol. In","x":0.080246911891201575,"c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.81069956461588544,"x":0.078189311677127671,"y":0.49411071841981657,"t":"humans, mutations of this gene are associated with increased risk of heart disease. Polar bears may","h":0.0175171886933847},{"y":0.51295641949672177,"t":"therefore be an important study model to understand heart disease in humans.","x":0.078189295897366742,"w":0.63991770426432282,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"The genome of the polar bear may also provide the solution for another condition, one that particularly","w":0.83768417358398439,"y":0.54609881203627997,"x":0.080127911961788145,"h":0.02147176493183045},{"h":0.022216347558478788,"c":1,"w":0.83766367594401059,"x":0.078087339840359676,"t":"affects our older generation: osteoporosis. This is a disease where bones show reduced density, usually","y":0.56241930172662857},{"x":0.078114972653902062,"h":0.019841516537436044,"w":0.81291732788085935,"y":0.58215001683314571,"t":"caused by insufficient exercise, reduced calcium intake or food starvation. Bone tissue is constantly","c":1},{"t":"being remodelled, meaning that bone is added or removed, depending on nutrient availability and the","y":0.60073014668907321,"h":0.018616421343440659,"c":1,"x":0.078129881966174153,"w":0.82521331787109387},{"w":0.81699467976888007,"t":"stress that the bone is under. Female polar bears, however, undergo extreme conditions during every","h":0.018803353865660566,"c":1,"x":0.078126920631777258,"y":0.61874304145015624},{"y":0.63526386757874287,"h":0.01969935054352201,"t":"pregnancy. Once autumn comes around, these females will dig maternity dens in the snow and will","x":0.078121812734627649,"w":0.81083730061848969,"c":1},{"h":0.019083393757419076,"w":0.82109781901041667,"c":1,"y":0.65395223394937974,"t":"remain there throughout the winter, both before and after the birth of their cubs. This process results","x":0.076080033248317511},{"h":0.018456115318271404,"x":0.078118365153343916,"y":0.6719822128973979,"w":0.81907679239908859,"t":"in about six months of fasting, where the female bears have to keep themselves and their cubs alive,","c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.01895698080074093,"t":"depleting their own calcium and calorie reserves. Despite this, their bones remain strong and dense.","w":0.81687240600585931,"y":0.69040697713243959,"x":0.078189311246628299},{"x":0.078124777295747669,"w":0.79643463134765624,"t":"Physiologists Alanda Lennox and Allen Goodship found an explanation for this paradox in 2008.","h":0.018556303916185346,"c":1,"y":0.72453061540341313},{"t":"They discovered that pregnant bears were able to increase the density of their bones before they","x":0.080246904666381819,"h":0.017667844522968212,"y":0.74263839808618093,"w":0.77983540852864575,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"started to build their dens. In addition, six months later, when they finally emerged from the den with","x":0.078131415206295851,"w":0.82727269490559896,"y":0.75994788478318021,"h":0.020203402803418702},{"h":0.015988372380376759,"c":1,"y":0.77906976729512067,"x":0.078189310301578802,"t":"their cubs, there was no evidence of significant loss of bone density. Hibernating brown bears do not","w":0.82304524739583329},{"c":1,"h":0.02026450311056438,"x":0.078109009255235076,"w":0.82320220947265621,"y":0.79399438625133101,"t":"have this capacity and must therefore resort to major bone reformation in the following spring. If the"},{"t":"mechanism of bone remodelling in polar bears can be understood, many bedridden humans, and even","x":0.078109586445860371,"y":0.81314999382122477,"w":0.83143000284830726,"h":0.019946925070035815,"c":1},{"t":"astronauts, could potentially benefit.","x":0.078189301487086971,"h":0.017667844522968212,"y":0.83127208485933346,"w":0.29835390726725258,"c":1},{"y":0.90552325561727054,"t":"16","w":0.024691357612609863,"h":0.014534883982160851,"x":0.080246913674054218,"c":1}]}